# MANIFEST — file-level ownership for the pipeline (decision D20, .icm/project.md; agency layer D23).
#
# One line per file, relative to <repo>/.icm/. The first column is the owner:
#   T  template-owned  — byte-identical in every pipeline repo. `icm-sync.sh --apply` overwrites
#                        the repo's copy from this folder; `icm-check.sh` reports it as drift
#                        until it does. Never carries a repo's identity — project specifics are
#                        read at runtime from `.icm/project.json` and `_shared/project-rules.md`.
#   P  project-owned   — seeded ONCE by `icm-check.sh --fix` when missing (the file here is the
#                        stub), then the repo's forever: neither script ever touches it again.
#
# Both scripts read this file; nothing outside it can ever be synced. Add a file here to have it
# seeded (and, for T, synced). The MANIFEST is itself template-owned and lands in every repo as
# `.icm/MANIFEST`, beside `.icm/template-version` (written by `icm-sync.sh --apply` and
# `setup.sh --fix`, never synced), so `setup.sh` can answer "complete? current?" offline, with no
# icm-board in reach. A file the template retires leaves this list and is REPORTED by
# `icm-sync.sh` for the operator to `git rm` — never deleted by a script (retired so far:
# `scripts/notify.sh`, replaced by `scripts/report.sh`).
#
# `skills/<name>/` (three seeded capability skills — `skills/README.md`) and `_shared/run-pack/`
# (the seven canonical run files `run-pack.sh` seeds into every run) are template-owned like the
# contracts: no identity, read `project.json` at runtime. A `skills/*/scripts/*.sh` is executable
# like `scripts/*.sh`; `scripts/lib/` stays sourced-only.
#
# A `T` file is changed in icm-board, never in a repo: a request to change one, made in a repo,
# is a template change request — `_shared/template-change.md` (itself T) says how it is written,
# parked and brought back. An in-repo edit is drift the next `--apply` overwrites.
